WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.767
Rate limiting.

2
00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:06.467
A rate limiter protects your wallet from users, and the provider's limits from you.

3
00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:10.267
The algorithm to know is the token bucket.

4
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:14.567
The bucket holds up to a burst of tokens, and refills at a steady rate.

5
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:17.103
Each request takes one token.

6
00:00:17.103 --> 00:00:22.500
If the bucket is empty, we reject with a 429 and a retry after header.

7
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.000
Watch the bucket level.

8
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.100
A full bucket lets a burst of requests through at once, and drains it.

9
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.953
Then it refills steadily.

10
00:00:30.953 --> 00:00:33.933
Burst allowed, but the average rate is enforced.

11
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:37.600
There is a stricter cousin, the sliding window.

12
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.833
The bucket allows bursts, while the window allows none.

13
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.800
The bucket refills steadily, while the window counts requests in the last few seconds.

14
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.133
A Redis sorted set implements the window naturally.

15
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:53.000
So pick the bucket for flexibility, and the window for strictness.

16
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.000
Here is a bucket stored in Redis.

17
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:01.633
We load the saved tokens, and add refill for the time that has passed, capped at the burst size.

18
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:04.633
If less than one token is left, we reject.

19
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.479
Otherwise we spend one token and save.

20
00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:11.900
In production, wrap this in a Lua script so it is one atomic step.

21
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.833
The Orbital Research Agent needs limits in both directions.

22
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:20.813
Inbound, a teammate's script sends twenty requests.

23
00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:27.233
Ten drain the bucket, and request eleven gets a clean 429 instead of a forty dollar bill.

24
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:31.033
Outbound, the DONKI API caps at thirty requests a minute.

25
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:38.167
A shared bucket across all four workers holds the fleet at twenty eight per minute, and the provider errors disappear.

26
00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:40.136
Let's recap.

27
00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:43.600
A token bucket allows bursts at a steady average rate.

28
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.533
An empty bucket means a 429.

29
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:48.500
A sliding window is stricter.

30
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.280
And limit both inbound and outbound.

31
00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.933
Next up: load balancing.
